{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"greisen-enjoyable-zethuswasp","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Finding\n\nFound while landing ADR 0117 (task `arterial-bilineate-vampirebat` / `a01e-01e7-6e6e-4809`) and\nS10 (`arbitrary-nautical-spreadwing` / `4530-95dc-11b2-45ac`). S10's scope is\n`.github/workflows/test.yml` only, so these two sites were left untouched and filed here rather\nthan folded into a reviewed plan that did not cover them.\n\n`.github/workflows/test.yml` was not the only place asserting that Windows is unsupported. The\nsame claim is stated twice more, in the fcntl gate and its own test:\n\n```\nscripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py:19-20\n    (actually *using* ``fcntl`` off POSIX is out of scope — Windows is not a declared\n    support target — only that import/collection succeeds.)\n\ntests/unit/test_fcntl_import_guard.py:12-13\n    Locking itself is not required to work off POSIX — Windows is not\n    a declared support target — only that import/collection succeeds.\n```\n\nADR 0117 makes both false. S10's proving grep does not catch them: it is scoped to `test.yml`, and\nthe wording differs (\"is not a declared\" vs \"not yet a declared\") and is line-wrapped across the\nphrase, so a naive tree-wide grep for the S10 sentence misses it too.\n\n## Why this is more than a stale comment\n\nThe sentence is not decoration — it is the gate's **scope statement**, and the scope it declares is\nexactly the one ADR 0117 reverses. The gate deliberately checks only that a module-scope\n`import fcntl` is guarded, so the package stays *importable* off POSIX, and explicitly disclaims any\ncheck that `fcntl` is *usable*. That was correct while Windows was unsupported. Under ADR 0117 it\nleaves the tree with a gate whose name suggests fcntl portability is covered while the failure mode\nthat actually produces the sweep's dominant class — a guarded import followed by an unguarded use —\nis out of its scope by design.\n\nThat gap is not hypothetical. It is why `_store/lock.py` (story `friendless-alabaster-cub`) and\n`reducer/marker.py` (bug `discrete-unrelative-vervet`) both shipped the identical defect while this\ngate passed on both files.\n\n## Scope\n\n**Do change:**\n- the scope sentence in `scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py` and the mirrored sentence in\n  `tests/unit/test_fcntl_import_guard.py`, to state that Windows IS a declared support target\n  (cite ADR 0117) and to say plainly what the gate does and does not check today.\n- decide, and record in the same change, whether the gate is **extended** from import-guard to\n  use-guard — flagging a module that binds `fcntl = None` on ImportError and then calls\n  `fcntl.<attr>` outside an `if fcntl is not None` / `sys.platform` branch.\n\n**Do NOT change:** `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` or `src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` (story\n`friendless-alabaster-cub` owns those and is in flight).\n\n## On extending the gate\n\nExtending it is the higher-value half, and it is a deterministic AST property of exactly the shape\nthe gate already implements, so it needs no CI provider and no LLM (`project.portability` is\nsatisfied). If it is extended, `reducer/marker.py` must be either fixed first or admitted with the\nexisting `# fcntl-guard-ok: <reason>` marker, because the gate is wired into `make lint` and would\notherwise fail the build on an unrelated change.\n\nIf the decision is NOT to extend it, that must be written down with its reason, so the next reader\ndoes not re-derive the same gap.\n\n## Suggested acceptance criteria\n\n1. Neither `scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py` nor `tests/unit/test_fcntl_import_guard.py` states\n   that Windows is not a declared support target; both cite ADR 0117.\n2. The gate's docstring states accurately what it checks (import guarding) and, explicitly, whether\n   it checks use guarding.\n3. If extended: a test proves the gate flags a guarded-import/unguarded-use module, and\n   `make lint` is green on the tree at the time of landing.\n4. If not extended: the reason is recorded in the gate's docstring, naming the defect class it\n   knowingly does not catch.\n","id":"bf74-a636-e6d9-40f9","parent_id":"55da-cee1-e556-4735","priority":2,"tags":["windows","portability","ci-gate","signal-quality","docs"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"The fcntl import-guard gate still documents Windows as out of scope, and only guards the import, not the use"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788573984679875219,"uuid":"cd8a5b91-39f5-46fd-9eb0-ac5ffec87d18"}